In a single-byte locale (C, POSIX) the prompt is printed wrong once a short line of
the history is recalled after one that wrapped, when the prompt has escape
sequences (PS1 with colours): bash53-017 (readline83-004) replaced the test whether
to print the prompt again from column 0 by one for multibyte locales, where
_rl_last_c_pos is a column; in a single-byte locale it is an index into the line,
as the test before took it. The single-byte test as before, the multibyte one as
bash53-017 has it.
The patch of Kerin Millar (23.10.2026) for readline 8.3.6 as Gentoo carries it
(sys-libs/readline files/readline-8.3_p6-single-byte-prompt-reprint.patch, bug
984299:
https://github.com/gentoo/gentoo/blob/master/sys-libs/readline/files/readline-8.3_p6-single-byte-prompt-reprint.patch),
the path that of the readline in bash; Gentoo's bash links the readline of the
system, this one is built with its own. The same line in the devel branch of bash
(a4a9dbc, 30.10.2026) is "pmt_offset + (...) ? _rl_last_c_pos :
local_prompt_len", which C reads as "(pmt_offset + (...)) ? ...", so not that.

--- lib/readline/display.c
+++ lib/readline/display.c
@@ -1509,7 +1509,11 @@ rl_redisplay (void)
 	         on the current screen line begins in the buffer. It is a
 	         buffer position, an index into curline
 	         (local_prompt + pmt_offset) */
-	      cursor_bufpos = pmt_offset + local_prompt_len;
+	      cursor_bufpos = pmt_offset;
+	      if (mb_cur_max == 1 || rl_byte_oriented)
+		cursor_bufpos += _rl_last_c_pos;
+	      else
+		cursor_bufpos += local_prompt_len;
 
 	      if (local_prompt && local_prompt_invis_chars[cursor_linenum] &&
 		    _rl_last_c_pos > 0 &&
